00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:19.950
Silicon Valley to learn from

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:42.690
Mayuko: So in order to start talking about this, we always
got to start with taking a little look back into history and

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:44.820
in this case, Japanese history.

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:56.900
Okay, but Japan's history is LONG. So this is going to be
one of the most like, shortened versions of it based on the

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:03.140
things that I found on the internet. Before World War Two,
Japan was going through an era called the Meiji Restoration.

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:08.960
This united the country under the Emperor Meiji with
strengthened it through its unification and basically

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:14.960
started Japan's Industrial Revolution. Before this era,
Japan was in a period called "sakoku" or like, closed

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:22.670
borders. So they didn't trade or talk to really any foreign
countries, except for a very small few. And so just like,

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:29.300
foreign influence just didn't happen in Japan for many, many
years. So because they weren't receiving any influence, they

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:36.530
were just like, behind in many aspects compared to the rest
of the world. Mind you. This is like the 1800s. So it really

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:43.610
wasn't that long ago. Well, this industrialization led to
militarization of Japan, which then led to World War Two,

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:49.910
which then they of course lost. After a period in which they
needed to rebuild their nation, they experienced just like

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:57.560
such a huge economic boom, which basically got them to have
one of the highest GDP per capita in the world. And this is

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:04.490
after the Japanese economy shifted its focus from things
like agriculture and mining, to telecommunications and

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:11.630
computers. This was when Japan had his technological
revolution, in my opinion. So much so, that in 1990, six of

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:18.380
the world's top 10, semiconductor companies were Japanese.
Intel actually ranked fourth between Japanese companies NEC,

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:27.740
Toshiba, and Hitachi. So yeah, Japan basically had like huge
ups and downs to ride in the 18th and 19th century. But now

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:34.430
when we think about the 21st century, when we think about
like, what technologies and tech companies are changing the

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:41.060
world, we often think about Silicon Valley and FAANG. And in
the meanwhile, there's many headlines about how Japan is

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:45.410
falling behind, especially in technology. So what happened?

00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:56.790
So based on what I knew about Japan, I had a few guesses.
Firstly, I realized that the whole like Japan's living in

00:05:56.790 --> 00:06:03.330
the future thing was not rooted in anything actually
technologically revolutionary. It instead was rooted in

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:10.110
convenience, and sharing a common set of values because of
how homogenous society was. So things like pristine

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:17.970
convenience stores and on time trains. And, like, you know,
when you go up an escalator in Japan, everybody stands on

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:24.270
one side together to allow the passer bys to go up. I think
all of that is the whole living in the future thing, but

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:30.750
it's just convenience. So then I thought about the working
culture, there's tons of videos and articles that talk about

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:37.230
the intensity of the work culture in Japan, one that kind of
like, even puts American Hustle culture to shame. (Not that

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:41.970
anyone should like have that title, or that's something to
like, fight for her or anything like that). But things like

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:48.570
overtime, poor work life balance, and a really rigid
hierarchical structure are things that I've heard about time

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:54.180
and time again. They were all in the context of Japanese
cultural values. So things like "meiwaku wo kakenai", like

00:06:54.180 --> 00:07:00.870
don't burden others, to being afraid of failure, as well as
risk aversion. Now, I'm not like a sociologist, or an

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:06.630
anthropologist, by any means. So I don't know where these
values came from. But these are ones that I've seen in my

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:12.810
own life, and I've seen echoed by other Japanese people that
I know. And I can totally see how all that impacts Japanese

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:19.590
working culture. So was that it? Was it the working culture?
Was that why ever since software and the internet started

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:32.550
dominating the world, Japanese products and services have
become increasingly absent from our lives? Well, what I

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:39.840
learned is that Japan just didn't adapt to software as well
as other places had, because Japan thought of software as

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:47.820
second class. Okay, this one blew my mind because like,
software engineers are like the new doctors and lawyers like

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:49.560
the 2010s and 2020s.

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:54.090
Eric: Like in America, there's like a lot of prestige that
comes with being a software engineer, like software

00:07:54.090 --> 00:07:59.820
development is not really super respected as a career, it's
more like something that's meant to be outsourced. And that

00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:05.310
you do for kind of a few years before becoming a manager,
like a product manager or consultant or something like that.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:11.370
It doesn't have like the prestige we have in the US, I would
